    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm looking for some advice on pallet stacking for our new high-density racking system in the warehouse. we're dealing with some pretty heavy industrial loads and i'm worried about stability when we stack them high. specifically, what's the best way to handle the center of gravity and any interlocking patterns you'd recommend to keep things from shifting?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a high-density racking system handling heavy loads, ensuring stability is key. It's crucial to maintain a low center of gravity by placing heavier items at the bottom and progressively lighter items at the top. Interlocking pallets are beneficial but avoid using progressive interlocking unless you're stacking only one pallet high; for greater heights, consider staggered blocking. Always use block skews at the ends, every three rows (or 90 degrees), and consistently throughout your blocking pattern to maintain stability and reduce shifting. Regularly inspect your warehouse for any signs of movement in your stacked pallets.",
